Taskmaster — third-party attributions
==================================

GPUI (https://github.com/zed-industries/zed)
--------------------------------------------
The GPUI crates used by Taskmaster are pinned to a specific Zed revision. Crates
marked Apache-2.0 by upstream remain under that license. Zed describes its
repository as primarily GPL-3.0-or-later with Apache-2.0 components where
marked.

Taskmaster replaces GPUI's disabled, no-op `ztracing` profiling surface with a small
independent Apache-2.0 compatibility implementation in `taskmaster/vendor/` so the
unused GPL logging/profiling implementation is not linked into release builds.

The vendored `gpui_macos` renderer is an Apache-2.0 component from the same
pinned revision, with the local changes described in `taskmaster/Cargo.toml`.

herdr (https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr)
-----------------------------------------
Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
A copy of the license is available at http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

The screen-detection predicates in the following files are ADAPTED from
herdr's TOML screen manifests (`src/detect/manifests/*.toml`):

    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/amp.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/antigravity.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/copilot.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/devin.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/droid.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/grok.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/hermes.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/kilo.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/kimi.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/kiro.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/opencode.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/pi.json
    Sources/TaskmasterCore/Resources/manifests/qoder.json

Changes made: the rules were translated from herdr's TOML rule schema into
Taskmaster's JSON predicate schema, re-prioritised for Taskmaster's evaluator (which
stops at the first matching rule by descending priority), restricted to the
screen regions Taskmaster implements, and paired with capture regions. Rules that
depend on herdr-only regions (`top_non_empty_lines`, `after_last_horizontal_rule`,
`after_last_prompt_marker`) were dropped rather than approximated.

The `"agent"` capability blocks in those files — binaries, resume specs,
session-id flags, approve/deny keystrokes, env hygiene, injection wiring — are
Taskmaster's own; herdr does not model most of them. Binary names and resume
argv were cross-checked against herdr's `src/detect/mod.rs` and
`src/agent_resume.rs`.

The manifests for claude-code, codex, cursor, gemini, generic, and shell are
Taskmaster's own and predate this attribution.

